Module visibility controls which declarations cross a module boundary. Ordinary declarations are private to their module. An `export:` block in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` marks what other modules can see.

### Same-module internal visibility

Files within the same module can bind each other's declarations directly through dependency clauses. A module is defined by its root directory. All `.moth` files in that directory (and its subdirectories that do not have their own root) belong to the same module.

Given this topology:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    @entry.moth
    components/
    ├── @mod.moth
    └── utils.moth
]

`utils.moth` and `@mod.moth` belong to the same `components` child module. Same-module dependency clauses can see ordinary helper declarations.

Internal helper:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- components/utils.moth
    internal_value #= 42

    internal_fn || -> String:
        return "internal"
    ;
]

Child root:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- components/@mod.moth
    @utils internal_value, internal_fn

    export:
        @utils internal_value
    ;
]

Same-module dependency clauses bypass the public-surface boundary. The declaring file sees helper declarations in the target file.

### Root-local declarations

A declaration authored directly in a root file belongs to that root only. It is not a shared module declaration:

- another root file cannot bind it
- ordinary helper files cannot bind it
- another root cannot re-export it
- naming a root file in a dependency path is rejected

The rejection reports the root-local declaration as a related site.

Helper declarations remain shared, prepared once with one origin. Each root binds them through ordinary dependency clauses. Same-spelling declarations in disjoint roots stay independent unless a bound shared helper already owns that name.

### Cross-module public boundary

A different module can only access declarations marked public by the applicable facade. Cross-module dependency clauses cannot bypass the public API.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- @entry.moth
    @components internal_value
]

The parent module sees only the `components` child module's `@mod.moth` surface. It cannot bypass that boundary with `@components/utils internal_value`. The `@components/utils` path cannot bypass the child facade.

`@mod.moth` publishes the normal surface. `@package.moth` publishes the scoped support surface. An entry-only child publishes no importable surface, so a normal dependency that requests one receives a missing-surface diagnostic. Resolution never falls back from one facade to another.

Without a matching export block, the cross-module dependency clause is rejected with `MOTH-IMPORT-0011`.

Implicit numeric failure stays module-local unless a declared error contract
delivers it. Private functions without an error slot may infer and propagate
it to same-module callers without postfix `!` at each internal call. An
exported callable without an error slot must discharge it. Public receiver
methods and generic functions obey the same closed contract, even when their
implementation later shares a backend with the caller. See
@../project-structure/#public-api (Public API) and @../progress (Progress).

### Normal modules are not source packages

An `@mod.moth` normal module is not a source package. Use `@package.moth` when the example teaches a scoped package. See @#project-local-packages (Project-local packages) for the support-package contract.

### Root-private declarations

Declarations in a root file outside its `export:` block are private to that root. They are not available to other modules even through the public surface.

### Direct root dependency rejection

Direct dependency clauses on `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth`, `@entry.moth` and `config.moth` are rejected. Depend on the module through its directory path, not through the root filename.

### Runtime top-level bindings are not bindable

Runtime top-level bindings are entry-body code in `@entry.moth`. They are not bindable declarations. Only compile-time constants, functions, structs, choices, type aliases and traits are bindable.

### Receiver method visibility

Receiver methods are part of their receiver type's public surface. A module that exports a receiver type also exposes that type's same-file methods. Any file that holds a value of the type can call those methods, even when it does not import or bind the type's name. Naming the type in an annotation, constructor, conformance or bound still needs an import or binding. Methods of a type the module keeps private are not callable from other modules. Export the receiver type's source surface or a wrapper explicitly.

### Dependency cycles

Circular dependency clauses between files in the same module are accepted when the declarations resolve in dependency order. Circular constant dependencies are rejected with `MOTH-RULE-0033`.

### No public access through filesystem tricks

There is no way to bypass the module public-surface boundary by choosing a different dependency path. All cross-module dependency clauses go through the same resolution and visibility check.

`export:` is the implemented public marker. A later `$export` migration is accepted direction, but block support remains undecided. The future spelling stays restricted to `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` and will not leave both forms as permanent alternatives.

### Concepts related to module visibility

- For the export block see @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API).
- For dependency path families see @#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
- For re-export syntax see @#public-reexports (Public re-exports).
